# Same as wipe_and_prep.seq, but for the wakeup-conflict walkthroughs that
# rely on TestBlockedSystemWakeupMinuteOffsetsPos/Neg (see main.c's `#ifdef
# APP_TEST_HOOKS` blocks around test_wakeup_schedule()/inbox_received()).
# These simulate another app's wakeup occupying specific minutes RELATIVE
# to whichever timer's own end_time is currently being evaluated - offset
# "0" means "that timer's own exact minute", re-centered fresh on every
# rearm_wakeup() call, so it keeps blocking the right thing even as a
# timer's own end_time shifts (+1 min, -1 min, ...) or as a DIFFERENT
# timer becomes the current target. Pos is a comma-separated list of
# minutes at/after that reference minute, Neg is minutes before it - most
# walkthroughs here only ever need Pos="0".
#
# EACH OFFSET ONLY BLOCKS ITS OWN EXACT MINUTE - list every minute you want
# occupied, don't space offsets 2 apart expecting the ±60s window to
# bridge the gap between them. Confirmed against the real firmware (not
# guessed): PebbleOS's actual exclusion check
# (prv_check_count_and_availability_callback, src/fw/services/normal/
# wakeup.c) is `(entry.timestamp - WAKEUP_EVENT_WINDOW < candidate) &&
# (candidate < entry.timestamp + WAKEUP_EVENT_WINDOW)` with
# WAKEUP_EVENT_WINDOW=60 (wakeup.h) - a STRICT, open interval on both
# sides. A candidate exactly 60s before or after an existing wakeup is
# NOT refused; the boundary itself is excluded. Since every offset here
# and every search candidate in main.c's own wakeup_find_early_slot()/
# wakeup_find_soonest_slot() are whole-minute-aligned, two adjacent
# minutes are always exactly 60s apart - i.e. exactly on that excluded
# boundary - so offset "0" alone does NOT also block minute "1" or "-1".
# test_wakeup_schedule() (main.c) reproduces this exact strict check
# (`diff > -60 && diff < 60`), not an approximation of it - a genuine
# other real wakeup wouldn't bridge that gap either, so to simulate an
# N-minute-wide occupied neighborhood, list every minute in it explicitly
# (e.g. "0,1,2" for 3 consecutive minutes), not "0,2".
#
# IMPORTANT: these hooks are compiled in only when the build was made with
# APP_TEST_HOOKS=1 in the environment (`APP_TEST_HOOKS=1 pebble build`, or
# exported before INSTALL's own implicit build runs). The message keys
# themselves are always declared (harmless, unused integers in a normal
# build), so sending them against a non-hooks build is NOT an error - it's
# just silently ignored, which looks exactly like "no conflict ever
# happened" in every screenshot that follows. If a walkthrough that
# imports this file seems to never show a conflict window at all, check
# this first before suspecting the feature itself.
KILL
WIPE
INSTALL
SLEEP 3

# Deliberately does NOT pin the emulator's own clock - see wipe_and_prep.seq's
# own comment on why (backward clock discontinuities from repinning
# corrupt any sequence that waits for a real timer to fire, which several
# wakeup_conflict_*.seq files that import this do). FreezeDisplay below is
# an app-side rendering override instead (see main.c's display_now()) -
# doesn't touch the real emulator/system clock, so it applies here
# identically and safely, independent of the APP_TEST_HOOKS machinery
# above.
APPMSG --int 10011=0 10029=1 10030=0
